WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.690
&gt;&gt; So, let's start.
Professor Vyas Sekar

00:00:06.691 --> 00:00:14.430
is associate Professor
at CMU EC department.

00:00:14.431 --> 00:00:16.685
He's part of the CyLab
and Conic Center.

00:00:16.686 --> 00:00:19.380
He works in networks and
security and he's talking

00:00:19.381 --> 00:00:23.710
about Universal Monitoring
and Universal Sketching.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.690
&gt;&gt; All right. Thank you.

00:00:28.691 --> 00:00:30.800
Thanks for having me
this summer school,

00:00:30.801 --> 00:00:33.100
seems like some fun
process are going on.

00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:35.240
So, this is actually
based on a talk I gave

00:00:35.241 --> 00:00:37.700
recently at ECM staff,

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.180
which is surprising that they

00:00:39.181 --> 00:00:41.140
invited a systems person
to a theory conference.

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:42.920
So, this is actually
an interesting story

00:00:42.921 --> 00:00:44.960
of serendipitous collaboration

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:46.865
between theory and systems.

00:00:46.866 --> 00:00:48.890
We'd originally started
off doing it in

00:00:48.891 --> 00:00:51.720
the context of
network monitoring,

00:00:51.721 --> 00:00:53.240
but thinking more broadly,

00:00:53.241 --> 00:00:56.210
I actually think that this type

00:00:56.211 --> 00:00:57.890
of work has broader applications

00:00:57.891 --> 00:01:00.290
to telemetry and analytics
and whatever kind of

00:01:00.291 --> 00:01:03.750
monitoring we want to do
in future IoT systems.

00:01:03.751 --> 00:01:06.210
Before I begin actually,
I want to thank

00:01:06.211 --> 00:01:08.495
a lot of people who actually
did most of this work.

00:01:08.496 --> 00:01:10.855
I'm just here as
an evangelist for this work.

00:01:10.856 --> 00:01:12.990
The original universal
monitoring team Alan Liu,

00:01:12.991 --> 00:01:16.020
who is a student
at Johns Hopkins,

00:01:16.021 --> 00:01:18.110
and his advisers
Vladimir Braverman,

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:19.450
who's behind most of

00:01:19.451 --> 00:01:22.910
this theoretical breakthroughs
in universal sketching.

00:01:22.911 --> 00:01:25.280
We've also done a lot
of implementations

00:01:25.281 --> 00:01:26.540
to show that it's not

00:01:26.541 --> 00:01:27.950
just theory but
there's also a lot of

00:01:27.951 --> 00:01:30.355
practical implementations
on different platforms.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:31.970
So, there's a bunch of
folks who have helped

00:01:31.971 --> 00:01:34.120
us do these things.

00:01:34.121 --> 00:01:36.720
All right. So, let's dive

00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:39.170
in to the motivation
for this work.

00:01:39.171 --> 00:01:40.580
So, the motivation for

00:01:40.581 --> 00:01:42.590
this work really stems
from the fact that,

00:01:42.591 --> 00:01:46.965
if you are managing
a large network system,

00:01:46.966 --> 00:01:49.125
an IoT system for example,

00:01:49.126 --> 00:01:53.290
you actually have a whole
different slew of analytics,

00:01:53.291 --> 00:01:56.920
tasks that you actually want
to run on top of the system.

00:01:56.921 --> 00:01:58.730
So, for example, I may want to

00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:00.020
estimate what kind of

00:02:00.021 --> 00:02:01.700
traffic is going
through the network,

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:02.840
I might want to estimate

00:02:02.841 --> 00:02:06.085
different properties of the
data through my network;

00:02:06.086 --> 00:02:09.830
I might want to check for
different kinds of anomalies.

00:02:09.831 --> 00:02:12.950
Has there been a spike
in a particular region?

00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:15.060
Are there new heavy-hitters

00:02:15.061 --> 00:02:17.625
while sending a lot of traffic,
and so on, and so forth?

00:02:17.626 --> 00:02:20.540
So, these are
the analytics tasks that I

00:02:20.541 --> 00:02:23.635
might be interested
in a network setting.

00:02:23.636 --> 00:02:27.500
Now, here's a very informal
problem definition

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:29.250
of what I would like to do.

00:02:29.251 --> 00:02:32.170
So, imagine an IoT deployment
or network deployment,

00:02:32.171 --> 00:02:34.160
I have a bunch of
different telemetry

00:02:34.161 --> 00:02:35.700
on monitoring applications,

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:39.430
and notice that the very fact
that I'm doing monitoring,

00:02:39.431 --> 00:02:42.150
means that many of these tasks
might be unforeseen.

00:02:42.151 --> 00:02:44.840
I may not know until
the deployment happens or

00:02:44.841 --> 00:02:45.950
until the system is actually

00:02:45.951 --> 00:02:48.605
running of what I'm looking for.

00:02:48.606 --> 00:02:50.750
Obviously, this problem is

00:02:50.751 --> 00:02:52.440
trivial if there are no
resource constraints.

00:02:52.441 --> 00:02:56.365
The key factors in a network
or an IoT deployment,

00:02:56.366 --> 00:02:58.250
there are key
resource constraints on

00:02:58.251 --> 00:03:00.380
your elements that are

00:03:00.381 --> 00:03:02.620
monitoring the state
of your system.

00:03:02.621 --> 00:03:04.540
So, these could be either CPU,

00:03:04.541 --> 00:03:07.155
or memory constraint, or
bandwidth constraint,

00:03:07.156 --> 00:03:08.870
and reporting the statistics

00:03:08.871 --> 00:03:12.050
to a central agent or
in an IoT setting,

00:03:12.051 --> 00:03:15.310
things like energy that becomes
very important as well.

00:03:15.311 --> 00:03:20.330
So, generic problem is

00:03:20.331 --> 00:03:23.345
really deciding what
monitoring primitives

00:03:23.346 --> 00:03:25.300
do I want to run on my sensors,

00:03:25.301 --> 00:03:28.080
on my monitors, on
my network routers,

00:03:28.081 --> 00:03:29.860
and so on, such that they

00:03:29.861 --> 00:03:31.940
respect these
resource constraints.

00:03:31.941 --> 00:03:35.540
They operate within
the system parameters but still

00:03:35.541 --> 00:03:38.750
provide high-fidelity
estimates or

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.970
provide high accuracy for the
applications I care about.

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:43.430
That if I actually
wanted to detect

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:44.870
an attack or detect if there is

00:03:44.871 --> 00:03:46.370
a spike in energy usage in

00:03:46.371 --> 00:03:47.900
a particular
environment and so on,

00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:49.280
I do want to

00:03:49.281 --> 00:03:52.060
provide high accuracy
for those applications.

00:03:52.061 --> 00:03:54.920
Alright. So, this is
the driving question

00:03:54.921 --> 00:03:55.670
for this work is what

00:03:55.671 --> 00:03:57.630
monitoring primitives
do I want it on?

00:03:57.631 --> 00:04:00.725
Now, again, I'm going
to use networking

00:04:00.726 --> 00:04:03.620
as a driving application
or a driving scenario,

00:04:03.621 --> 00:04:04.790
but you can think this has more

00:04:04.791 --> 00:04:06.415
broader applications as well.

00:04:06.416 --> 00:04:10.730
So, the traditional approach
you would think about doing

00:04:10.731 --> 00:04:15.230
this is as data comes to
you in a streaming model,

00:04:15.231 --> 00:04:17.690
the very simplest thing
you can do is just run

00:04:17.691 --> 00:04:20.280
a uniform sampling algorithm,

00:04:20.281 --> 00:04:21.755
and then collect the data,

00:04:21.756 --> 00:04:23.150
and then send it to
some central agent

00:04:23.151 --> 00:04:25.170
for running estimates
on top of that.

00:04:25.171 --> 00:04:28.920
So, for example, in the context
of network monitoring,

00:04:28.921 --> 00:04:31.640
the most common used primitive

00:04:31.641 --> 00:04:34.370
today is something
called Netflow,

00:04:34.371 --> 00:04:38.520
or Cflow, or
other industry terms.

00:04:38.521 --> 00:04:40.025
So what it does is it

00:04:40.026 --> 00:04:42.710
samples the incoming
packets at random,

00:04:42.711 --> 00:04:44.840
groups packets with
the similar pattern

00:04:44.841 --> 00:04:46.160
into what are called flows,

00:04:46.161 --> 00:04:48.420
and then reports these flows and

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:51.480
their counters to
some central collection point.

00:04:51.481 --> 00:04:53.265
So, here's a quick animation.

00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:55.160
You have different flows here;

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:56.820
flows one, three, and six.

00:04:56.821 --> 00:04:58.560
Flow one has five packets,

00:04:58.561 --> 00:04:59.610
flow three as one packet, flow

00:04:59.611 --> 00:05:00.895
six has one packet, and so on.

00:05:00.896 --> 00:05:04.115
As these items come
through the system,

00:05:04.116 --> 00:05:05.630
this monitor is sampling

00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:07.570
then discarding some
of the samples,

00:05:07.571 --> 00:05:09.865
updating contours for
a subset of these,

00:05:09.866 --> 00:05:13.325
and then it reports
whatever it sampled.

00:05:13.326 --> 00:05:15.200
The sample flow reports to

00:05:15.201 --> 00:05:16.310
some central agent and then I

00:05:16.311 --> 00:05:18.080
can estimate different properties

00:05:18.081 --> 00:05:20.000
of the stream that I wanted to

00:05:20.001 --> 00:05:22.765
monitor using the sample
reports I've collected.

00:05:22.766 --> 00:05:25.220
So, this is a very
typical workflow that

00:05:25.221 --> 00:05:28.870
happens in most
monitoring tasks today.

00:05:28.871 --> 00:05:32.345
Now, the problem is and
many people have observed it

00:05:32.346 --> 00:05:35.600
is sampling is
a reasonable starting point,

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.460
but it is actually not great for

00:05:37.461 --> 00:05:39.985
a number of fine-grained tasks.

00:05:39.986 --> 00:05:41.870
For example, if I wanted
to do anomaly detection,

00:05:41.871 --> 00:05:43.490
or some security monitoring,

00:05:43.491 --> 00:05:46.525
or finding the heavy hitter
that's in the network,

00:05:46.526 --> 00:05:49.380
sampling may actually not
give you the right answer.

00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:51.845
Now, an alternative approach,

00:05:51.846 --> 00:05:55.460
and there's been a lot of
really cool theoretical work

00:05:55.461 --> 00:05:57.980
in this space is what are called

00:05:57.981 --> 00:06:00.320
sketching or
streaming algorithms.

00:06:00.321 --> 00:06:01.580
So, in a general workflow

00:06:01.581 --> 00:06:03.200
here is think about, it is like,

00:06:03.201 --> 00:06:06.355
if you tell me what metric
you are interested in,

00:06:06.356 --> 00:06:07.790
like saying, I want to find

00:06:07.791 --> 00:06:10.170
the maximum value
of a data stream,

00:06:10.171 --> 00:06:12.460
or I want to find the median
value of a data stream,

00:06:12.461 --> 00:06:14.000
or I want to find the percentiles

00:06:14.001 --> 00:06:15.170
of these values in a data stream,

00:06:15.171 --> 00:06:19.470
and so on, if you tell
me the metric a priori,

00:06:19.471 --> 00:06:21.665
there are a number of very cool,

00:06:21.666 --> 00:06:24.080
compact, highly
efficient algorithms,

00:06:24.081 --> 00:06:26.015
or sketching algorithms
that can maintain

00:06:26.016 --> 00:06:28.625
a compressed data
structure that are

00:06:28.626 --> 00:06:30.140
extremely optimized for

00:06:30.141 --> 00:06:31.945
monitoring that
particular metric.

00:06:31.946 --> 00:06:33.640
So, if I say, "I want
to find heavy-hitters,

00:06:33.641 --> 00:06:35.190
I want to find the
entropy of this stream,

00:06:35.191 --> 00:06:36.695
I want to detect people

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:38.370
talking to a number
of destinations,

00:06:38.371 --> 00:06:39.860
and so on, and so forth,

00:06:39.861 --> 00:06:41.450
I can actually go and develop

00:06:41.451 --> 00:06:42.740
these custom algorithms deployed

00:06:42.741 --> 00:06:44.325
them on my monitoring nodes,

00:06:44.326 --> 00:06:46.430
collect the data structures

00:06:46.431 --> 00:06:48.505
relevant for that particular
monitoring tasks,

00:06:48.506 --> 00:06:50.060
and then report them back."

00:06:50.061 --> 00:06:53.105
So, there's been a lot
of fascinating theory

00:06:53.106 --> 00:06:57.805
in this literature that
solve these problems.

00:06:57.806 --> 00:07:00.830
Now, of course, from a practical
systems point of view,

00:07:00.831 --> 00:07:02.960
imagine you're
developing an IoT sensor

00:07:02.961 --> 00:07:05.150
or an IoT monitoring system,

00:07:05.151 --> 00:07:08.040
this is not attainable or
a practical trajectory.

00:07:08.041 --> 00:07:09.905
The set of metrics
I care about in

00:07:09.906 --> 00:07:12.320
the stream is always
constantly growing,

00:07:12.321 --> 00:07:14.360
and I also don't know if
priori what metrics I

00:07:14.361 --> 00:07:16.870
want to monitor
the state of my network.

00:07:16.871 --> 00:07:19.935
So, clearly from
a systems point of view,

00:07:19.936 --> 00:07:22.370
this is not a great idea
because it entails

00:07:22.371 --> 00:07:24.020
higher complexity on your sensor

00:07:24.021 --> 00:07:26.420
with limited compute
and bandwidth.

00:07:26.421 --> 00:07:28.400
It's obviously
higher development time

00:07:28.401 --> 00:07:29.900
like everyone of us
has to go develop

00:07:29.901 --> 00:07:33.110
new algorithms as new applications
emerge, and moreover,

00:07:33.111 --> 00:07:36.420
even if you think about
a re-programmable or

00:07:36.421 --> 00:07:38.480
a re-flashable sensor that you

00:07:38.481 --> 00:07:41.095
can actually change
the algorithms running on it.

00:07:41.096 --> 00:07:43.160
Even that is not sufficient

00:07:43.161 --> 00:07:46.200
because at any given
point of time,

00:07:46.201 --> 00:07:49.190
the resources on
that node have been

00:07:49.191 --> 00:07:52.280
tightly committed to the metric
of interest already.

00:07:52.281 --> 00:07:54.560
If I want to do some retroactive
analysis and ask, "Hey,

00:07:54.561 --> 00:07:55.950
what was the value of this metric

00:07:55.951 --> 00:07:57.925
in one five minutes ago?"

00:07:57.926 --> 00:07:59.840
Unless that metric was
already being monitored,

00:07:59.841 --> 00:08:01.305
I actually don't know that value.

00:08:01.306 --> 00:08:04.190
So, it's not very good
for time series analysis,

00:08:04.191 --> 00:08:06.700
or attractive forensics,
and so on, and so forth.

00:08:06.701 --> 00:08:10.230
So, this dilemma,

00:08:10.231 --> 00:08:13.815
this two classes of
estimation approaches,

00:08:13.816 --> 00:08:15.680
generic approaches
like sampling which

00:08:15.681 --> 00:08:18.410
are okay but not very accurate,

00:08:18.411 --> 00:08:20.030
or custom algorithms
that are highly

00:08:20.031 --> 00:08:23.190
accurate but entail
a lot of complexity.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.315
From a systems point of
view, this question has

00:08:26.316 --> 00:08:28.715
been bugging people
for a long time.

00:08:28.716 --> 00:08:30.650
Interestingly, the same problem

00:08:30.651 --> 00:08:32.805
has also been bugging
the theory community.

00:08:32.806 --> 00:08:36.515
So, the theory community is
asking a similar question in

00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:38.550
this open question from

00:08:38.551 --> 00:08:42.730
a theory workshop on
this question of,

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:48.290
"Is there a vision for a
universal sketching solution?"

00:08:48.291 --> 00:08:50.330
That is, rather than commit

00:08:50.331 --> 00:08:52.610
your sketch to a single function,

00:08:52.611 --> 00:08:54.335
is it possible to define

00:08:54.336 --> 00:08:56.780
these sketching
primitives that are

00:08:56.781 --> 00:09:01.150
generic across a wide range
of possible functions?

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:05.599
So, this is an open
question back in 2011,

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:06.920
and people didn't
really know there is

00:09:06.921 --> 00:09:09.755
an answer to this
question, and so on.

00:09:09.756 --> 00:09:14.840
So, based on the ideal vision
that we want for developing

00:09:14.841 --> 00:09:16.880
a robust monitoring
infrastructure

00:09:16.881 --> 00:09:19.265
for future IoT deployments
and future deployments,

00:09:19.266 --> 00:09:20.540
it's what I would call the vision

00:09:20.541 --> 00:09:22.130
for universal monitoring.

00:09:22.131 --> 00:09:24.380
The idea here is

00:09:24.381 --> 00:09:26.045
as you're stream of data

00:09:26.046 --> 00:09:27.370
goes through
your monitoring node,

00:09:27.371 --> 00:09:29.090
I can do some amount

00:09:29.091 --> 00:09:31.435
of data processing in
a streaming fashion.

00:09:31.436 --> 00:09:33.500
I want to keep as generic

00:09:33.501 --> 00:09:35.150
a computation or
a data structure as

00:09:35.151 --> 00:09:38.655
possible on what I call
the monitoring data plane,

00:09:38.656 --> 00:09:41.050
the hardware, the IoT sensors.

00:09:41.051 --> 00:09:44.180
I report this generic or
compressed data structure

00:09:44.181 --> 00:09:46.370
back to some central
collection agents,

00:09:46.371 --> 00:09:48.745
so something in the management
or control plane.

00:09:48.746 --> 00:09:52.370
The idea and the hope is that
whatever I am collecting

00:09:52.371 --> 00:09:53.645
can be used for

00:09:53.646 --> 00:09:56.105
a variety of downstream
applications or task.

00:09:56.106 --> 00:09:57.200
Somebody else can go write

00:09:57.201 --> 00:10:00.790
queries on top of this compressed
data structure and say,

00:10:00.791 --> 00:10:03.200
"I want to re-estimate
or recover properties

00:10:03.201 --> 00:10:06.625
at the stream that my
application really cares about."

00:10:06.626 --> 00:10:08.630
So, again this is

00:10:08.631 --> 00:10:11.750
one data structure for
a wide variety of tasks,

00:10:11.751 --> 00:10:13.010
and the nice thing is actually

00:10:13.011 --> 00:10:16.600
naturally what I would
call Late-binding,

00:10:16.601 --> 00:10:17.990
and this is a nice
property to have

00:10:17.991 --> 00:10:20.450
from a systems perspective,

00:10:20.451 --> 00:10:22.210
is that when I
deploy this sensor,

00:10:22.211 --> 00:10:24.290
build this hardware,
investment my

00:10:24.291 --> 00:10:26.920
developer's time and
deploying these algorithms,

00:10:26.921 --> 00:10:29.840
I do not need to know a priori
what my customers want.

00:10:29.841 --> 00:10:33.154
So, this is actually a
very powerful, practical,

00:10:33.155 --> 00:10:35.600
architecture I do not
need to know what

00:10:35.601 --> 00:10:36.680
the customer wants before

00:10:36.681 --> 00:10:38.680
deploying them out there
in the real world.

00:10:38.681 --> 00:10:41.190
So, this is a crazy vision.

00:10:41.191 --> 00:10:42.980
I mean, it's not clear
how I can do it,

00:10:42.981 --> 00:10:44.000
so it's obviously a bunch of

00:10:44.001 --> 00:10:46.190
different technical challenges

00:10:46.191 --> 00:10:48.500
in this problem space
that we actually

00:10:48.501 --> 00:10:51.030
don't know the answers
to at this time.

00:10:51.031 --> 00:10:53.335
So, a bunch of open questions.

00:10:53.336 --> 00:10:55.125
First of all, there's

00:10:55.126 --> 00:10:56.765
this theoretical question of does

00:10:56.766 --> 00:10:58.925
a universal sketch
actually exist?

00:10:58.926 --> 00:11:01.875
This is an open question, does
the sketch actually exist?

00:11:01.876 --> 00:11:03.420
Even if it does,

00:11:03.421 --> 00:11:04.860
it may be impractical
to implement.

00:11:04.861 --> 00:11:05.840
So what's the point of having

00:11:05.841 --> 00:11:07.640
a theoretically elegant algorithm

00:11:07.641 --> 00:11:09.230
if it's not very practical to

00:11:09.231 --> 00:11:10.880
implement on
a resource constraint,

00:11:10.881 --> 00:11:12.070
a hardware, or a router,

00:11:12.071 --> 00:11:13.580
and so on? Yeah.

00:11:13.581 --> 00:11:16.585
&gt;&gt; What is the definition
of a universal sketch?

00:11:16.586 --> 00:11:17.790
&gt;&gt; So, the definition of

00:11:17.791 --> 00:11:18.910
universal sketch is exactly this.

00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:20.535
You give me a set of functions,

00:11:20.536 --> 00:11:22.070
or set of metrics,

00:11:22.071 --> 00:11:23.780
or applications you want to run.

00:11:23.781 --> 00:11:27.830
I have to put one single
counting data structure on

00:11:27.831 --> 00:11:29.705
my monitor such that

00:11:29.706 --> 00:11:31.955
all of those applications
will have high accuracy.

00:11:31.956 --> 00:11:33.260
&gt;&gt; But I thought your example

00:11:33.261 --> 00:11:35.705
was come up an application
in the future?

00:11:35.706 --> 00:11:36.215
&gt;&gt; Correct.

00:11:36.216 --> 00:11:36.690
&gt;&gt; And the.

00:11:36.691 --> 00:11:38.650
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:11:38.651 --> 00:11:39.830
So, the setup applications might

00:11:39.831 --> 00:11:41.670
be unknown or unforeseen,

00:11:41.671 --> 00:11:44.060
but you may bound them and

00:11:44.061 --> 00:11:44.900
say they may come from

00:11:44.901 --> 00:11:46.090
this family of
functions and so on.

00:11:46.091 --> 00:11:50.095
We will qualify what that
universality means in a second.

00:11:50.096 --> 00:11:51.290
But think about the way

00:11:51.291 --> 00:11:52.960
there are any number
of tasks that

00:11:52.961 --> 00:11:54.750
a future network operator or

00:11:54.751 --> 00:11:57.800
a future IoT analytics framework
may think about,

00:11:57.801 --> 00:12:00.030
and the counters and keeping

00:12:00.031 --> 00:12:01.050
should be sufficient to

00:12:01.051 --> 00:12:03.340
estimate that
particular application.

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:08.060
Sorry. So, again,

00:12:08.061 --> 00:12:09.510
the idea there is you have
resource constraints.

00:12:09.511 --> 00:12:10.520
They don't have
infinite memory, They

00:12:10.521 --> 00:12:11.810
don't have infinite processing.

00:12:11.811 --> 00:12:14.370
So, it has to be
a compact data structure.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:18.330
Sorry? So, typically,
the sketching and

00:12:18.331 --> 00:12:20.349
the streaming algorithms
are sub-linear

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.550
in terms of the number
of items in the stream,

00:12:22.551 --> 00:12:23.660
and I'll define
the streaming problem

00:12:23.661 --> 00:12:25.145
a little bit better.

00:12:25.146 --> 00:12:27.080
Obviously, fired like
infinite things,

00:12:27.081 --> 00:12:29.355
I would dump all the data,
send out the data.

00:12:29.356 --> 00:12:31.670
But that means that I'm
violating the CPU consumption,

00:12:31.671 --> 00:12:34.370
the memory consumption, and
the communication cost.

00:12:34.371 --> 00:12:36.100
That's not a viable option for

00:12:36.101 --> 00:12:39.800
IoT routers or
anything in practice.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.030
So, the first question is,

00:12:43.031 --> 00:12:44.720
does a universal sketch exist?

00:12:44.721 --> 00:12:45.830
Before I go there,

00:12:45.831 --> 00:12:46.800
I should give a little bit of

00:12:46.801 --> 00:12:48.140
background to formally define

00:12:48.141 --> 00:12:50.650
what I mean by a sketching
or a streaming algorithm,

00:12:50.651 --> 00:12:53.100
and again, I'm going
to use some of

00:12:53.101 --> 00:12:54.130
the standard terminology from

00:12:54.131 --> 00:12:56.695
the theory community to explain
what these problems are.

00:12:56.696 --> 00:13:01.235
So, here's a very quick
one-slide overview

00:13:01.236 --> 00:13:03.795
of what a stream or a
sketching algorithm means.

00:13:03.796 --> 00:13:05.890
So, you have a data stream.

00:13:05.891 --> 00:13:07.955
A stream is of length m,

00:13:07.956 --> 00:13:09.470
say the number of
packets or the number

00:13:09.471 --> 00:13:12.340
of sensor readings
that you're getting,

00:13:12.341 --> 00:13:14.985
and there are n
unique items, say,

00:13:14.986 --> 00:13:16.390
unique IP addresses or

00:13:16.391 --> 00:13:20.190
unique values of
energy readings and so on.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:25.210
So, I want to use
a small amount of memory,

00:13:25.211 --> 00:13:27.120
in terms of the size
of the stream.

00:13:27.121 --> 00:13:30.440
I want to use something like

00:13:30.441 --> 00:13:34.000
sublinear in m or n. Sketches are

00:13:34.001 --> 00:13:36.400
essentially a class of
these data-stream summaries

00:13:36.401 --> 00:13:38.270
that intuitively do

00:13:38.271 --> 00:13:40.210
some kind of hash-based
computation or

00:13:40.211 --> 00:13:43.595
some compressed data
structure of the stream.

00:13:43.596 --> 00:13:45.985
So, one way to think about
it, say for example,

00:13:45.986 --> 00:13:48.910
at least for me intuitively is,

00:13:48.911 --> 00:13:50.920
zero to order problem is

00:13:50.921 --> 00:13:53.885
checking whether you
saw this item before.

00:13:53.886 --> 00:13:56.115
So, this Bloom filter, you
can think of as a sketch.

00:13:56.116 --> 00:13:59.550
It's a very simple sketch
that lets you do set

00:13:59.551 --> 00:14:01.130
membership queries
without actually

00:14:01.131 --> 00:14:03.055
having to store every
item you've seen so far.

00:14:03.056 --> 00:14:06.215
So, think about sketches
or data streams,

00:14:06.216 --> 00:14:09.060
streaming algorithms
are extensions

00:14:09.061 --> 00:14:11.505
to a Bloom filter approach,

00:14:11.506 --> 00:14:13.205
a data structure that
also lets you do

00:14:13.206 --> 00:14:15.170
volumetric or counting queries,

00:14:15.171 --> 00:14:16.805
not just membership queries.

00:14:16.806 --> 00:14:18.280
Again, it's very loose

00:14:18.281 --> 00:14:19.600
hand-wavy way of
thinking about a sketch.

00:14:19.601 --> 00:14:20.730
If you've never seen
a sketch before,

00:14:20.731 --> 00:14:22.330
think of it as like some sort of

00:14:22.331 --> 00:14:27.160
a Bloom filter plus
counting capabilities.

00:14:27.161 --> 00:14:29.200
There are actually a lot of

00:14:29.201 --> 00:14:32.370
seminal theory results that
talk about the feasibility of

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:34.170
doing these kind of computations
for different kinds

00:14:34.171 --> 00:14:37.825
of metrics of interest
on your screen.

00:14:37.826 --> 00:14:40.955
One of the earliest
request was in '85,

00:14:40.956 --> 00:14:42.810
the AMS paper in '96 actually won

00:14:42.811 --> 00:14:48.195
the Golden Prize in Theoretical
Computing. All right.

00:14:48.196 --> 00:14:51.630
So, typically, in the standard
sketching formulation,

00:14:51.631 --> 00:14:53.330
the way the theory has progressed

00:14:53.331 --> 00:14:55.200
is you define
a particular metric,

00:14:55.201 --> 00:14:56.310
say for example, I want to

00:14:56.311 --> 00:14:58.620
find the number of
unique items in a stream,

00:14:58.621 --> 00:15:00.600
I want to find
the first order moment,

00:15:00.601 --> 00:15:02.720
I want to find the second moment
of a stream, and so on.

00:15:02.721 --> 00:15:06.820
So, given a particular metric,

00:15:06.821 --> 00:15:08.490
there's a particular
sketch that is

00:15:08.491 --> 00:15:10.590
probably optimal in
terms of the memory,

00:15:10.591 --> 00:15:11.890
accuracy, trade-offs

00:15:11.891 --> 00:15:14.475
for that particular
metric of interest.

00:15:14.476 --> 00:15:17.040
So, I have one algorithm
that solves one problem,

00:15:17.041 --> 00:15:19.010
and that is the classical
way of thinking

00:15:19.011 --> 00:15:22.025
about sketching or
streaming algorithms.

00:15:22.026 --> 00:15:24.770
Now, universality or
universal question,

00:15:24.771 --> 00:15:26.590
universal streaming I'm
asking here, and again,

00:15:26.591 --> 00:15:27.765
going back to your question

00:15:27.766 --> 00:15:29.920
about what do I mean
by universality,

00:15:29.921 --> 00:15:30.840
this is a little bit of

00:15:30.841 --> 00:15:32.405
a restricted version
of universality,

00:15:32.406 --> 00:15:37.820
which is that if you think
about the frequency vector,

00:15:37.821 --> 00:15:40.920
which is the frequency of item i,

00:15:40.921 --> 00:15:43.810
many of the existing sketches
I think about is you

00:15:43.811 --> 00:15:46.610
can write them as either
summation of f_i squared,

00:15:46.611 --> 00:15:49.450
summation f_i cubed,
summation f_i or f_i log,

00:15:49.451 --> 00:15:50.665
f_i, and so on, and so forth.

00:15:50.666 --> 00:15:54.030
There are some functions
of the frequency vector.

00:15:54.031 --> 00:15:55.640
Now, we are asking,

00:15:55.641 --> 00:15:58.000
universality is really about

00:15:58.001 --> 00:16:00.110
can I estimate an arbitrary G,

00:16:00.111 --> 00:16:01.900
summation of the G of f_i?

00:16:01.901 --> 00:16:03.980
Some G function
that I don't know,

00:16:03.981 --> 00:16:05.785
but I'm restricted
to this class of

00:16:05.786 --> 00:16:07.750
functions where G
comes in this form.

00:16:07.751 --> 00:16:09.265
Let my function is of this form,

00:16:09.266 --> 00:16:12.540
expressed as a summation
of the frequency vectors.

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:25.530
So, this is a classic,

00:16:25.531 --> 00:16:26.880
this is theoretical
formulation of it.

00:16:26.881 --> 00:16:28.490
So, one of the things we've

00:16:28.491 --> 00:16:30.620
found is many things that we
actually want in practice,

00:16:30.621 --> 00:16:31.750
actually mapped to that.

00:16:31.751 --> 00:16:32.910
So, I have to think about,

00:16:32.911 --> 00:16:34.820
whether the particular
function you're thinking

00:16:34.821 --> 00:16:37.190
about can be mapped into
this particular things.

00:16:37.191 --> 00:16:38.860
Actually, I tell you
the theoretical result.

00:16:38.861 --> 00:16:40.360
So, then we can see
whether, it actually

00:16:40.361 --> 00:16:41.980
fits that particular form or not,

00:16:41.981 --> 00:16:43.900
or maybe there's
an approximate version

00:16:43.901 --> 00:16:44.840
of the function
you're writing that

00:16:44.841 --> 00:16:46.395
actually might be
mapping to that.

00:16:46.396 --> 00:16:48.230
But, that's actually
a very good open question,

00:16:48.231 --> 00:16:48.740
which I don't know.

00:16:48.741 --> 00:16:50.690
It's like if you have
an application in mind,

00:16:50.691 --> 00:16:52.115
does it fit my model?

00:16:52.116 --> 00:16:53.595
That I don't know,

00:16:53.596 --> 00:16:55.180
I can't just give you
an intuitive answer as like,

00:16:55.181 --> 00:16:57.350
"Hey, this is there or not."

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:02.240
So, here's an example,
you can write this,

00:17:02.241 --> 00:17:05.075
many of the classical sketches
like first-order,

00:17:05.076 --> 00:17:07.170
unique elements,
entropy sketches,

00:17:07.171 --> 00:17:08.910
all of them can be
written in this form,

00:17:08.911 --> 00:17:12.765
as some kind of G-sum function.

00:17:12.766 --> 00:17:15.120
So, and this is where

00:17:15.121 --> 00:17:20.440
this somewhat
counter-intuitive result.

00:17:20.441 --> 00:17:22.965
In theory, that
actually says that

00:17:22.966 --> 00:17:25.980
indeed there exists
a universal approach or

00:17:25.981 --> 00:17:28.390
a universal data structure
or a universal sketch

00:17:28.391 --> 00:17:32.895
that can support a very
large family of functions.

00:17:32.896 --> 00:17:36.570
So, these are class of

00:17:36.571 --> 00:17:39.795
functions that intuitively don't

00:17:39.796 --> 00:17:41.740
grow faster than f_i squared.

00:17:41.741 --> 00:17:43.695
So, if the function
does not grow faster,

00:17:43.696 --> 00:17:45.475
monotonically faster
than f_i squared,

00:17:45.476 --> 00:17:46.690
it turns out there is

00:17:46.691 --> 00:17:49.010
a universal sketch
construction for

00:17:49.011 --> 00:17:52.525
that particular class
of functions.

00:17:52.526 --> 00:17:55.865
The theory that's
an extra bit universality proof,

00:17:55.866 --> 00:17:57.500
and then the existential proof
is like there is

00:17:57.501 --> 00:17:59.450
a universal sketch that
exists that actually

00:17:59.451 --> 00:18:01.910
can be used to estimate
this G-sum with

00:18:01.911 --> 00:18:04.790
high probability using
polylogarithmic memory,

00:18:04.791 --> 00:18:08.115
again, sublinear memory in
terms of the stream size.

00:18:08.116 --> 00:18:10.950
Now, again, I did not
come up with this theory,

00:18:10.951 --> 00:18:12.150
it was really cool theory,

00:18:12.151 --> 00:18:14.100
and probably beyond
my comprehension,

00:18:14.101 --> 00:18:15.600
but it's actually
a very counter-intuitive

00:18:15.601 --> 00:18:17.690
and interesting result that shows

00:18:17.691 --> 00:18:20.335
the feasibility of developing

00:18:20.336 --> 00:18:23.760
a universal construction for
a large family of functions.

00:18:23.761 --> 00:18:25.300
Again, as I said,

00:18:25.301 --> 00:18:26.570
many things we cared about in

00:18:26.571 --> 00:18:27.980
the network setting seemed to

00:18:27.981 --> 00:18:31.275
fit this definition
very naturally.

00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:36.310
&gt;&gt; So, one option is to just keep
every f_i [inaudible] usually?

00:18:36.311 --> 00:18:37.240
&gt;&gt; As a sketch, yeah.

00:18:37.241 --> 00:18:39.745
&gt;&gt; So, I assume that this
does better than that?

00:18:39.746 --> 00:18:41.670
&gt;&gt; Actually, you'll see
some other results,

00:18:41.671 --> 00:18:43.360
I think one of the nice things of

00:18:43.361 --> 00:18:45.600
universality is actually
this late binding,

00:18:45.601 --> 00:18:47.090
and I don't need
to know it prior,

00:18:47.091 --> 00:18:48.575
that what is necessary,

00:18:48.576 --> 00:18:51.000
and also the nice thing
also we'll see is actually

00:18:51.001 --> 00:18:54.920
that it's better to
give more memory,

00:18:54.921 --> 00:18:57.590
the union of the memory to
like a universal sketch,

00:18:57.591 --> 00:19:00.415
than fragment your resources
across multiple sketches.

00:19:00.416 --> 00:19:02.530
So, one of the the subset

00:19:02.531 --> 00:19:03.440
from both systems and

00:19:03.441 --> 00:19:04.755
theoretical perspectives
is actually interesting,

00:19:04.756 --> 00:19:07.245
actually says that we'll
see as the portfolio grows,

00:19:07.246 --> 00:19:08.570
it's better to give the resources

00:19:08.571 --> 00:19:09.760
to a universal sketch and

00:19:09.761 --> 00:19:11.790
fragment it across
custom sketches

00:19:11.791 --> 00:19:13.840
for each different application.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:19.095
Again, this G can be more
than just f_i, f_i squared.

00:19:19.096 --> 00:19:21.895
It can be a little bit
more broader than that.

00:19:21.896 --> 00:19:25.580
So, here is the intuitively
I've requested a theory,

00:19:25.581 --> 00:19:27.270
people to explain this

00:19:27.271 --> 00:19:28.725
to me a little bit
more intuitively.

00:19:28.726 --> 00:19:30.540
Intuitively, the way
to think about it is

00:19:30.541 --> 00:19:33.240
it goes back to
classical heavy hitters.

00:19:33.241 --> 00:19:35.020
Heavy hitters are items

00:19:35.021 --> 00:19:36.800
in the stream that
appear a lot of times,

00:19:36.801 --> 00:19:38.930
say the one person
who's getting the dust,

00:19:38.931 --> 00:19:40.630
or the one person that's
sending a lot of traffic,

00:19:40.631 --> 00:19:42.485
or heavy hitters in your stream.

00:19:42.486 --> 00:19:44.820
Now, intuitively,
you can essentially

00:19:44.821 --> 00:19:48.090
split the stream into two cases,

00:19:48.091 --> 00:19:52.695
either there is indeed
one very large g-heavy hitter,

00:19:52.696 --> 00:19:54.290
not a heavy hitter in
the classical sense,

00:19:54.291 --> 00:19:57.130
but a heavy hitter that
contributes to the G-sum.

00:19:57.131 --> 00:19:58.750
In which case, you can use

00:19:58.751 --> 00:20:01.930
a classical heavy hitter
algorithm and you're done.

00:20:01.931 --> 00:20:04.295
You basically get a very
accurate estimate of the G-sum,

00:20:04.296 --> 00:20:05.820
if you just find
the heavy hitters,

00:20:05.821 --> 00:20:06.975
the L2 heavy hitters.

00:20:06.976 --> 00:20:09.590
L2 here, the L2
norm of the stream.

00:20:09.591 --> 00:20:11.260
Now, the second case
is more interesting.

00:20:11.261 --> 00:20:13.940
It's actually suppose the
stream is such that there is

00:20:13.941 --> 00:20:16.090
no such sufficiently
large heavy hitter

00:20:16.091 --> 00:20:18.455
that's contributing
a significant amount of mass,

00:20:18.456 --> 00:20:20.025
then there is this trick

00:20:20.026 --> 00:20:21.850
where you can
actually think about

00:20:21.851 --> 00:20:24.900
running the recursive version of

00:20:24.901 --> 00:20:28.530
this on progressively
smaller sampled substreams,

00:20:28.531 --> 00:20:31.340
and in fact that's a logarithmic
number of substreams,

00:20:31.341 --> 00:20:32.500
and it turns out you can actually

00:20:32.501 --> 00:20:34.850
prove that finding heavy hitters

00:20:34.851 --> 00:20:37.155
on recursively sampled streams

00:20:37.156 --> 00:20:39.330
is sufficient to
estimate the G-sum.

00:20:39.331 --> 00:20:41.410
So, again, this is

00:20:41.411 --> 00:20:42.890
a very hand-wavy explanation of

00:20:42.891 --> 00:20:45.420
why universal sketching
actually works.

00:20:45.421 --> 00:20:47.990
So, let me actually show you what

00:20:47.991 --> 00:20:50.645
the data structure
actually looks like.

00:20:50.646 --> 00:20:53.205
So, what we're going to
do is, given a stream,

00:20:53.206 --> 00:20:55.640
we're going to use a number
of a logarithmic number

00:20:55.641 --> 00:20:57.930
of zero-one hash
functions, essentially,

00:20:57.931 --> 00:21:01.120
say whether or not
this particular item

00:21:01.121 --> 00:21:03.610
in the stream is being sampled.

00:21:03.611 --> 00:21:05.230
So, I've leveled like level zero,

00:21:05.231 --> 00:21:06.450
level one dot, dot,

00:21:06.451 --> 00:21:07.660
dot until level log(n),

00:21:07.661 --> 00:21:09.955
and at every level,

00:21:09.956 --> 00:21:13.310
I'm going to run
a classical heavy hitter sketch.

00:21:13.311 --> 00:21:15.325
So, this can be
Count-Min, Count-Sketch,

00:21:15.326 --> 00:21:16.740
any number of algorithms that you

00:21:16.741 --> 00:21:18.310
can plug-in and play here.

00:21:18.311 --> 00:21:19.450
So, any number of

00:21:19.451 --> 00:21:21.320
classical heavy hitter
algorithms can be plugged

00:21:21.321 --> 00:21:25.150
in to this framework that
does L2 heavy hitters.

00:21:25.151 --> 00:21:27.900
Then, I get the heavy hitters

00:21:27.901 --> 00:21:30.425
and their respective counters
from every level,

00:21:30.426 --> 00:21:33.350
and it turns out that
it's sufficient to get

00:21:33.351 --> 00:21:34.540
a very accurate estimate of

00:21:34.541 --> 00:21:39.940
a G-sum through
a recursive computation. Now.

00:21:39.941 --> 00:21:41.720
So, this is actually what the,

00:21:41.721 --> 00:21:43.450
intuitively, the data
structure looks like.

00:21:43.451 --> 00:21:44.870
If you think about

00:21:44.871 --> 00:21:47.580
our original vision of
universal monitoring, really,

00:21:47.581 --> 00:21:49.860
what we're going to do is put

00:21:49.861 --> 00:21:52.635
this sampling and sketch

00:21:52.636 --> 00:21:55.705
counting heavy-hitter sketches
in our data plane.

00:21:55.706 --> 00:21:57.680
So, there's a bunch of
hash function computations

00:21:57.681 --> 00:22:00.080
and these sketching counters.

00:22:00.081 --> 00:22:01.630
I'm going to push

00:22:01.631 --> 00:22:04.470
all the application
specific computation, say,

00:22:04.471 --> 00:22:06.375
I want to do data detection,

00:22:06.376 --> 00:22:09.030
I want to find the top
K people in the stream,

00:22:09.031 --> 00:22:10.250
I want to estimate the entropy of

00:22:10.251 --> 00:22:12.285
a particular dimension and so on,

00:22:12.286 --> 00:22:14.030
I'm going to push all
of those into what

00:22:14.031 --> 00:22:15.710
I would call the
logical control plane,

00:22:15.711 --> 00:22:17.520
some sort of analytics agent,

00:22:17.521 --> 00:22:20.260
or a cloud back-end or whatever.

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:23.955
So, all these, in
some sense are offline.

00:22:23.956 --> 00:22:26.585
Think about all the application
specific computation.

00:22:26.586 --> 00:22:27.980
That means they may
still be real-time,

00:22:27.981 --> 00:22:30.380
but they're not running
on the IoT sensor itself,

00:22:30.381 --> 00:22:33.760
they're running in
some back-end agent. Yeah.

00:22:33.761 --> 00:22:41.140
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:22:41.141 --> 00:22:47.060
&gt;&gt; Great question. So, we'll
get to that in a little bit.

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:55.800
So, you can think about
it that way as well.

00:22:55.801 --> 00:22:58.160
But that's actually one
of the things where

00:22:58.161 --> 00:22:59.300
the "theory meets
the practice" has

00:22:59.301 --> 00:23:00.625
to be a little bit more careful,

00:23:00.626 --> 00:23:02.670
and we give you examples
where, in some cases,

00:23:02.671 --> 00:23:03.870
memory is the bottleneck,

00:23:03.871 --> 00:23:05.705
and then in that case, that's
the right thing to do.

00:23:05.706 --> 00:23:07.740
In some cases where processing

00:23:07.741 --> 00:23:09.130
or CPU becomes the bottleneck,

00:23:09.131 --> 00:23:10.540
you may have to come
up with alternatives

00:23:10.541 --> 00:23:11.590
or approximations to

00:23:11.591 --> 00:23:14.750
that particular login-
the hash computation itself.

00:23:14.751 --> 00:23:16.870
In fact, we have found
that in some cases.

00:23:16.871 --> 00:23:19.825
I'll tell you about
that. All right,

00:23:19.826 --> 00:23:21.280
so this comes back to your

00:23:21.281 --> 00:23:22.765
prefetching the second question.

00:23:22.766 --> 00:23:24.350
Okay, I've told you
the universal sketch

00:23:24.351 --> 00:23:25.760
construction at a high level,

00:23:25.761 --> 00:23:27.825
is it actually
practical to implement?

00:23:27.826 --> 00:23:30.530
I don't yet have
experience implementing

00:23:30.531 --> 00:23:32.650
on low-power IoT platforms yet,

00:23:32.651 --> 00:23:36.860
and that's where I would like
to see this implemented,

00:23:36.861 --> 00:23:38.200
but I can talk about a couple of

00:23:38.201 --> 00:23:41.040
different experiences
that we've had in

00:23:41.041 --> 00:23:45.175
implementing on more classical
network-centric platforms.

00:23:45.176 --> 00:23:46.470
So, if we've implemented in

00:23:46.471 --> 00:23:49.970
an emerging programmable
software router,

00:23:49.971 --> 00:23:51.795
or switch architecture called P4,

00:23:51.796 --> 00:23:54.480
we've done it on other
network processor platforms,

00:23:54.481 --> 00:23:57.860
and also looked at
virtual switching platforms

00:23:57.861 --> 00:23:59.540
that are very common
in data centers.

00:23:59.541 --> 00:24:00.810
For example,
everything in Azure is

00:24:00.811 --> 00:24:02.130
running on top of
a virtual switch,

00:24:02.131 --> 00:24:03.720
so you can imagine running

00:24:03.721 --> 00:24:05.720
telemetry or monitoring
applications inside

00:24:05.721 --> 00:24:08.820
the virtual switch inside
your data centers, and so on.

00:24:08.821 --> 00:24:10.740
The cool thing for us is most of

00:24:10.741 --> 00:24:12.680
these systems are
already open source,

00:24:12.681 --> 00:24:15.010
and our implementation
to us as well.

00:24:15.011 --> 00:24:18.570
Okay, so this highlights, again,

00:24:18.571 --> 00:24:20.250
this interesting mapping of

00:24:20.251 --> 00:24:21.820
the theoretical
construction to practice,

00:24:21.821 --> 00:24:23.390
and there we have
to be a little bit

00:24:23.391 --> 00:24:25.335
careful about how we
do the implementation.

00:24:25.336 --> 00:24:28.830
So, here's our mapping to P4,

00:24:28.831 --> 00:24:31.380
and the classical way,

00:24:31.381 --> 00:24:33.605
the naive way of
doing it is to say,

00:24:33.606 --> 00:24:35.520
the entire data plane goes to P4,

00:24:35.521 --> 00:24:37.090
and the only
application estimation

00:24:37.091 --> 00:24:38.680
goes to the control plane,

00:24:38.681 --> 00:24:40.600
but it turns out
this actually was

00:24:40.601 --> 00:24:42.934
not the right thing to do for P4,

00:24:42.935 --> 00:24:46.225
especially because
the sketching layer

00:24:46.226 --> 00:24:47.690
requires you to do some sort of

00:24:47.691 --> 00:24:49.750
a priority queue or

00:24:49.751 --> 00:24:52.170
a sorting operation
inside the data plane,

00:24:52.171 --> 00:24:54.020
and that turns out to be
beyond the capabilities

00:24:54.021 --> 00:24:56.225
of what this P4 hardware
can actually do.

00:24:56.226 --> 00:24:59.155
So, what we did was,
as I essentially said,

00:24:59.156 --> 00:25:02.080
we made a pragmatic
choice to say well,

00:25:02.081 --> 00:25:03.110
we can't do a priority queue or

00:25:03.111 --> 00:25:04.965
a sorting operation
in the data plane.

00:25:04.966 --> 00:25:06.830
Instead, we'll just sacrifice

00:25:06.831 --> 00:25:08.530
a little bit more
communication overhead,

00:25:08.531 --> 00:25:10.140
send the raw reports,

00:25:10.141 --> 00:25:11.940
the raw data structure to
the control plane and say,

00:25:11.941 --> 00:25:14.185
you do the sorting part, I
can't do the sorting part.

00:25:14.186 --> 00:25:15.780
So, we have to be

00:25:15.781 --> 00:25:17.390
careful about how we
map this computation,

00:25:17.391 --> 00:25:19.680
and this was an
interesting exercise that

00:25:19.681 --> 00:25:21.495
showed the naive way of

00:25:21.496 --> 00:25:23.660
mapping into the hardware and
may not be the optimal way.

00:25:23.661 --> 00:25:25.220
So we sort of pushed

00:25:25.221 --> 00:25:27.595
the sorting part to
the control plane as well.

00:25:27.596 --> 00:25:29.835
But again, this is
a conscious trade-off,

00:25:29.836 --> 00:25:31.995
we incur a little bit more
of a communication overhead,

00:25:31.996 --> 00:25:34.165
a few megabytes more for
a very large string,

00:25:34.166 --> 00:25:37.460
but it's actually feasible
to map into hardware.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:40.915
So, this is in
the experience with

00:25:40.916 --> 00:25:44.215
this re-programmable
switch architecture.

00:25:44.216 --> 00:25:44.870
The other thing, actually,

00:25:44.871 --> 00:25:45.800
I want to point out is, actually,

00:25:45.801 --> 00:25:46.920
we've also implemented it

00:25:46.921 --> 00:25:52.060
in software switching or
software router platforms.

00:25:52.061 --> 00:25:54.765
Here, this is where

00:25:54.766 --> 00:25:55.980
some of the questions
you asked about,

00:25:55.981 --> 00:25:58.070
is it possible to compute

00:25:58.071 --> 00:26:00.520
these login hashes and
so on, comes into play.

00:26:00.521 --> 00:26:03.200
So, it turns out in
this software router

00:26:03.201 --> 00:26:05.230
or software switching platforms,

00:26:05.231 --> 00:26:06.980
memory is not a problem.

00:26:06.981 --> 00:26:08.960
You actually have a lot
of memory on your server.

00:26:08.961 --> 00:26:10.290
The problem is really that

00:26:10.291 --> 00:26:11.680
the number of times
you touch this packet,

00:26:11.681 --> 00:26:12.900
the number of
computations, or number of

00:26:12.901 --> 00:26:15.340
cycles per packet,
is what kills you.

00:26:15.341 --> 00:26:18.330
So, that's where we
actually realize that look,

00:26:18.331 --> 00:26:20.370
we will not be able to

00:26:20.371 --> 00:26:22.690
handle line rate computation

00:26:22.691 --> 00:26:24.950
using these classical
sketch implementations,

00:26:24.951 --> 00:26:27.650
using on this commodity
software switches

00:26:27.651 --> 00:26:29.500
on commodity servers.

00:26:29.501 --> 00:26:31.845
So, even the classical sort of

00:26:31.846 --> 00:26:34.070
the systems tricks like use DPDK,

00:26:34.071 --> 00:26:35.590
use kernel bypass, all
of those things are

00:26:35.591 --> 00:26:37.355
not sufficient to
solve this problem,

00:26:37.356 --> 00:26:40.385
you have to do something
a little bit more rigorous.

00:26:40.386 --> 00:26:42.630
There are ways to solve
this problem if you

00:26:42.631 --> 00:26:44.735
can do heuristics and so on,

00:26:44.736 --> 00:26:46.615
but they're not
elegant solutions.

00:26:46.616 --> 00:26:47.905
So, our idea was actually,

00:26:47.906 --> 00:26:51.400
we reformulated the sketching
problem for software and

00:26:51.401 --> 00:26:52.730
said if we can change

00:26:52.731 --> 00:26:55.040
the theory problem slightly
and say, "Hey, look,

00:26:55.041 --> 00:26:57.300
I'm willing to sacrifice
a little bit more memory,

00:26:57.301 --> 00:27:00.890
but I want a reduce of CPU,"
or "I'm actually willing to

00:27:00.891 --> 00:27:04.880
tolerate some delay
in the answer,

00:27:04.881 --> 00:27:06.400
and I can wait for
some convergence

00:27:06.401 --> 00:27:07.725
before I get an
accurate estimate."

00:27:07.726 --> 00:27:11.665
So, we can trade off delays
and trade off more memory,

00:27:11.666 --> 00:27:13.750
to actually reduce
the CPU dramatically.

00:27:13.751 --> 00:27:16.400
With these sort of tricks,
we actually can get really,

00:27:16.401 --> 00:27:17.850
really high rate, like say, 40,

00:27:17.851 --> 00:27:20.790
close to 40 gig on
a single core on

00:27:20.791 --> 00:27:22.450
a software switching platform

00:27:22.451 --> 00:27:26.005
with some additional
heuristics as well.

00:27:26.006 --> 00:27:29.140
Again, so the point of
this is to say that look,

00:27:29.141 --> 00:27:30.650
heuristic sketching
is a great idea,

00:27:30.651 --> 00:27:32.090
but if you give me
a particular platform,

00:27:32.091 --> 00:27:35.640
I need to spend some systems
engineering and careful,

00:27:35.641 --> 00:27:37.520
even theory re-tweaking,
to actually

00:27:37.521 --> 00:27:39.650
make it practical for
a particular platform.

00:27:39.651 --> 00:27:41.070
So, if I think about

00:27:41.071 --> 00:27:43.190
an IoT platform that

00:27:43.191 --> 00:27:45.280
has energy constraints or
communication constraints,

00:27:45.281 --> 00:27:46.600
I may need to tweak this further.

00:27:46.601 --> 00:27:48.320
So, I need to think about,

00:27:48.321 --> 00:27:49.800
in an IoT platform, for example,

00:27:49.801 --> 00:27:51.770
I imagine most of

00:27:51.771 --> 00:27:53.870
the energy actually goes
in the radio layer.

00:27:53.871 --> 00:27:56.300
Communication is vertical
and kills the battery

00:27:56.301 --> 00:27:58.615
on most of these devices I think.

00:27:58.616 --> 00:28:00.480
So we actually have to be
a little bit more cognizant,

00:28:00.481 --> 00:28:01.870
maybe CPU and memory
is not the problem,

00:28:01.871 --> 00:28:03.870
but communication cost
is the problem,

00:28:03.871 --> 00:28:05.350
and we may need to compress
the data structure

00:28:05.351 --> 00:28:08.005
further before you send it
to the controller and so on.

00:28:08.006 --> 00:28:13.130
I've not done that, that
is speculative. All right.

00:28:13.131 --> 00:28:16.150
So, so far I've told you there is

00:28:16.151 --> 00:28:17.695
a universal sketch and

00:28:17.696 --> 00:28:19.710
at least this preliminary
evidence on a couple of

00:28:19.711 --> 00:28:21.410
different platforms
suggests there is

00:28:21.411 --> 00:28:23.200
a feasible road-map
to implementing

00:28:23.201 --> 00:28:24.860
them and sort of realizing the

00:28:24.861 --> 00:28:27.200
theoretical benefits
that it promises.

00:28:27.201 --> 00:28:29.980
Now, obviously, one question
is why not just

00:28:29.981 --> 00:28:32.340
do custom sketches for
a bunch of different metrics?

00:28:32.341 --> 00:28:34.695
Is it actually competitive

00:28:34.696 --> 00:28:36.210
versus running a custom sketch

00:28:36.211 --> 00:28:38.150
for every single application?

00:28:38.151 --> 00:28:40.740
So, we did a number of

00:28:40.741 --> 00:28:43.900
trace-driven evaluations
to actually understand,

00:28:43.901 --> 00:28:45.165
is a universal sketch

00:28:45.166 --> 00:28:47.930
competitive with respect
to a custom sketch for,

00:28:47.931 --> 00:28:51.105
say, F1, F2, F3, and
so on, and so forth.

00:28:51.106 --> 00:28:53.500
Now, the first result here,

00:28:53.501 --> 00:28:55.905
the y-axis is the error rate,

00:28:55.906 --> 00:28:58.810
and the x-axis is
different tasks, heavy hitters,

00:28:58.811 --> 00:29:00.740
data's detection,
change detection,

00:29:00.741 --> 00:29:03.110
entropy, estimation, and so on.

00:29:03.111 --> 00:29:07.645
So, we give the same amount
of memory to a custom sketch,

00:29:07.646 --> 00:29:10.420
as well as same amount of
memory to a universal sketch,

00:29:10.421 --> 00:29:12.500
and we check how much off,

00:29:12.501 --> 00:29:13.600
in terms of the error

00:29:13.601 --> 00:29:14.855
of this particular
task, you are at.

00:29:14.856 --> 00:29:16.350
Of course, we have ground truth

00:29:16.351 --> 00:29:17.940
where we can do
exact computations and we

00:29:17.941 --> 00:29:21.160
compare it to the ground truth,
and the error gap,

00:29:21.161 --> 00:29:22.790
I mean, so, obviously, the
custom sketch is going to do

00:29:22.791 --> 00:29:24.435
better because it's
only doing one thing,

00:29:24.436 --> 00:29:25.780
it's doing this one thing well,

00:29:25.781 --> 00:29:28.280
but the gap is not
obscenely high.

00:29:28.281 --> 00:29:30.800
The gap is around like
less than four-person,

00:29:30.801 --> 00:29:34.705
which is something tolerable
given this amount of memory.

00:29:34.706 --> 00:29:38.255
So, notice that if you actually
give what I'm doing here,

00:29:38.256 --> 00:29:39.940
is slightly unfair to
universal sketching

00:29:39.941 --> 00:29:42.440
because if I built
a system with four tasks,

00:29:42.441 --> 00:29:44.770
I would need to do 600 plus
600 plus 600 plus 600,

00:29:44.771 --> 00:29:47.415
2400 MB for the
customer algorithm,

00:29:47.416 --> 00:29:49.895
but universal sketch only
gets 600 KB overall.

00:29:49.896 --> 00:29:51.610
So, it's a little bit
of an unfair comparison

00:29:51.611 --> 00:29:53.840
to universal sketch in this case.

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:56.940
Now, here's a more
interesting result,

00:29:56.941 --> 00:29:59.575
is what happens when I don't
have just a single app,

00:29:59.576 --> 00:30:00.760
but I have a portfolio of

00:30:00.761 --> 00:30:03.240
different estimation
applications I care about.

00:30:03.241 --> 00:30:05.640
This is where things get
interesting, where let's say,

00:30:05.641 --> 00:30:07.770
if I compare against
the portfolio of applications,

00:30:07.771 --> 00:30:10.600
and we try different
portfolios Appset1,

00:30:10.601 --> 00:30:12.115
Appset2 and Appset3
on the x-axis,

00:30:12.116 --> 00:30:13.255
and you actually realize,

00:30:13.256 --> 00:30:17.620
you see that as the
application portfolio grows,

00:30:17.621 --> 00:30:19.280
there is a clear win
for universal sketch.

00:30:19.281 --> 00:30:21.950
In fact, the accuracy of a
universal sketch becomes

00:30:21.951 --> 00:30:23.590
better because it's
actually getting

00:30:23.591 --> 00:30:25.620
more memory across the portfolio.

00:30:25.621 --> 00:30:27.370
It's better to use
the memory with

00:30:27.371 --> 00:30:28.820
the universal sketch
than fragmented

00:30:28.821 --> 00:30:30.595
for individual tasks.

00:30:30.596 --> 00:30:32.430
Of course, there are
still some tasks on

00:30:32.431 --> 00:30:34.320
which custom sketch
is doing better,

00:30:34.321 --> 00:30:35.600
but overall, the trend

00:30:35.601 --> 00:30:39.400
is in favor of
universal sketch approach.

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:46.825
So, that's where we are in
terms of universal sketches.

00:30:46.826 --> 00:30:48.745
Now, there's a whole bunch
of open questions

00:30:48.746 --> 00:30:50.325
that I don't know the answers to,

00:30:50.326 --> 00:30:53.660
especially if you think
about an IoT environment

00:30:53.661 --> 00:30:54.830
or a system-wide environment

00:30:54.831 --> 00:30:56.960
where there are multiple sensors,

00:30:56.961 --> 00:30:58.580
multiple dimensions,

00:30:58.581 --> 00:31:01.630
hierarchical dimensions
of interest and so on.

00:31:01.631 --> 00:31:04.110
So, I'll sort of briefly
define those problems

00:31:04.111 --> 00:31:05.020
and say I don't actually know

00:31:05.021 --> 00:31:06.265
the answers to these problems.

00:31:06.266 --> 00:31:10.740
So, in a more general
system-wide setting,

00:31:10.741 --> 00:31:13.210
you can think of the data
you are getting, actually,

00:31:13.211 --> 00:31:16.350
is a high-dimensional data
flowing through your network.

00:31:16.351 --> 00:31:18.330
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:31:18.331 --> 00:31:19.815
&gt;&gt; That is typically
for one dimension.

00:31:19.816 --> 00:31:23.375
Yeah. So, think about like
uni-dimensional dataset

00:31:23.376 --> 00:31:25.435
that's flowing
through your system.

00:31:25.436 --> 00:31:27.280
So, now you can think about

00:31:27.281 --> 00:31:30.230
a high-dimensional
data stream where,

00:31:30.231 --> 00:31:31.885
say, even in the network case,

00:31:31.886 --> 00:31:33.740
you have IP address,

00:31:33.741 --> 00:31:35.060
destination address,
source address,

00:31:35.061 --> 00:31:37.170
destination address, source
port, destination port,

00:31:37.171 --> 00:31:39.450
protocol and so on, there's
like five dimensions already,

00:31:39.451 --> 00:31:41.130
and you can think about

00:31:41.131 --> 00:31:43.490
a sensor deployment where

00:31:43.491 --> 00:31:46.410
you're collecting many more
dimensions of interest.

00:31:46.411 --> 00:31:48.550
In the more general case,

00:31:48.551 --> 00:31:52.335
you actually have a number
of these sensing locations.

00:31:52.336 --> 00:31:55.140
You have many nodes and many
dimensions of interest.

00:31:55.141 --> 00:31:56.635
Now the question then becomes,

00:31:56.636 --> 00:32:00.425
how do I actually map
this multi-dimensional problem

00:32:00.426 --> 00:32:05.795
and spacial-temporal
problem to this setting.

00:32:05.796 --> 00:32:09.090
Now, a trivial assertions
solution might be to place

00:32:09.091 --> 00:32:10.260
every sketch for every

00:32:10.261 --> 00:32:11.820
single dimension on
every single node,

00:32:11.821 --> 00:32:13.120
but that's obviously not

00:32:13.121 --> 00:32:14.500
the most optimal thing
you can do.

00:32:14.501 --> 00:32:15.730
We can do a little bit better,

00:32:15.731 --> 00:32:18.570
so we actually showed
where we can do a

00:32:18.571 --> 00:32:19.590
little bit better in terms of

00:32:19.591 --> 00:32:21.715
placement and coordination
across the sketches,

00:32:21.716 --> 00:32:23.845
at least for one

00:32:23.846 --> 00:32:26.560
where the case of the number
of dimensions is small.

00:32:26.561 --> 00:32:29.100
A more open question is,

00:32:29.101 --> 00:32:31.515
when you get to the
multi-dimensional question,

00:32:31.516 --> 00:32:33.955
you may be able to exploit

00:32:33.956 --> 00:32:35.895
some kind of
hierarchical structure

00:32:35.896 --> 00:32:37.430
in the data to
actually do better,

00:32:37.431 --> 00:32:39.220
and we're not doing any
of that stuff right

00:32:39.221 --> 00:32:42.090
now in the
multi-dimensional problem.

00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:46.040
The holy grail here
is to think about

00:32:46.041 --> 00:32:48.885
having one global monitor that
all the data goes through,

00:32:48.886 --> 00:32:51.170
and can I get the equivalent
accuracy of having

00:32:51.171 --> 00:32:53.435
all the data streaming
through one global node,

00:32:53.436 --> 00:32:56.195
even though I have
a system-wide deployment.

00:32:56.196 --> 00:32:58.600
I don't quite know
how to do that yet.

00:32:58.601 --> 00:33:01.450
That's the holy grail of

00:33:01.451 --> 00:33:06.150
this system-wide placement
and analytics problem.

00:33:06.590 --> 00:33:09.270
&gt;&gt; So that's where

00:33:09.271 --> 00:33:10.410
we are in terms of
universal sketching.

00:33:10.411 --> 00:33:13.180
I actually think that it
is a very cool primitive

00:33:13.181 --> 00:33:15.960
that can have
very broad applications

00:33:15.961 --> 00:33:18.055
not just in networking but
also in the IOT domain,

00:33:18.056 --> 00:33:19.450
especially going
forward where there's

00:33:19.451 --> 00:33:21.060
going to be many diverse tasks

00:33:21.061 --> 00:33:22.650
and many different kinds

00:33:22.651 --> 00:33:24.595
of estimates you
actually want to do.

00:33:24.596 --> 00:33:26.140
And this could actually be a

00:33:26.141 --> 00:33:28.485
fundamental building block
of how you build

00:33:28.486 --> 00:33:31.240
future analytics or
telemetric capabilities

00:33:31.241 --> 00:33:33.500
for IOT and so on.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:38.345
I actually briefly touched
upon a couple of other areas

00:33:38.346 --> 00:33:42.095
that are relevant to
this workshop agenda.

00:33:42.096 --> 00:33:43.100
One is talking about

00:33:43.101 --> 00:33:45.600
some interesting machine
learning problems

00:33:45.601 --> 00:33:47.360
at the intersection
of machine learning

00:33:47.361 --> 00:33:49.455
and IOT security and privacy.

00:33:49.456 --> 00:33:52.185
I have to talk about
some of these problems.

00:33:52.186 --> 00:33:54.260
I also talk a little bit about

00:33:54.261 --> 00:33:57.840
this new large initiative

00:33:57.841 --> 00:33:59.810
we are launching at
CMU called CONIX,

00:33:59.811 --> 00:34:02.700
which is talking about
a broader umbrella

00:34:02.701 --> 00:34:05.545
for many of these different
open problems in general.

00:34:05.546 --> 00:34:09.800
So, again, a set of
interesting- again,

00:34:09.801 --> 00:34:11.435
this is not meant to
be comprehensive but

00:34:11.436 --> 00:34:13.350
just to pique your interest about

00:34:13.351 --> 00:34:15.500
interesting problems
or applications of

00:34:15.501 --> 00:34:16.580
machine learning to security and

00:34:16.581 --> 00:34:18.710
privacy in this particular space.

00:34:18.711 --> 00:34:22.750
So I'm interested in looking at,

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:25.800
can we model the behavior

00:34:25.801 --> 00:34:27.730
of an IOT device
and automatically

00:34:27.731 --> 00:34:29.680
extract a behavioral model for

00:34:29.681 --> 00:34:32.275
what a normal behavior of
an IOT device is going to be.

00:34:32.276 --> 00:34:35.740
So the motivation here is many of

00:34:35.741 --> 00:34:36.800
the high-profile hacks and

00:34:36.801 --> 00:34:38.190
many of the high-profile
incidents we see

00:34:38.191 --> 00:34:39.315
today as somebody who

00:34:39.316 --> 00:34:40.745
went and broke into
your IOT device.

00:34:40.746 --> 00:34:42.790
There's a camera there,
somebody hacked into the camera

00:34:42.791 --> 00:34:46.040
and maybe my stream is
going out somewhere else.

00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:48.545
Now, one question is, well,

00:34:48.546 --> 00:34:51.450
these IOT devices are not
general-purpose computers.

00:34:51.451 --> 00:34:53.790
So, somebody using them as
general purpose computer is

00:34:53.791 --> 00:34:56.210
a behavioral aberration from

00:34:56.211 --> 00:34:57.530
the normal expected behavior of

00:34:57.531 --> 00:34:59.755
a thermostat or
a camera and so on.

00:34:59.756 --> 00:35:01.845
So, the question then
becomes can we build

00:35:01.846 --> 00:35:04.060
robust behavioral models that

00:35:04.061 --> 00:35:06.775
capture the footprint
of an IOT device?

00:35:06.776 --> 00:35:10.700
And use it to flag for
security violations and so on?

00:35:10.701 --> 00:35:12.210
Now, the challenge, of course,

00:35:12.211 --> 00:35:13.710
is how do I do it in

00:35:13.711 --> 00:35:15.985
the presence of
noisy and unlabeled data?

00:35:15.986 --> 00:35:18.250
So there's a disconnect

00:35:18.251 --> 00:35:19.955
between machine learning
and security in general.

00:35:19.956 --> 00:35:21.550
So, machine learning typically

00:35:21.551 --> 00:35:22.890
assumes that you have
somebody's nicely

00:35:22.891 --> 00:35:24.270
gave you a labeled data
set at least

00:35:24.271 --> 00:35:26.050
many classical
formulations of it though.

00:35:26.051 --> 00:35:27.250
In security, we actually don't

00:35:27.251 --> 00:35:28.530
have these labels, and often,

00:35:28.531 --> 00:35:30.210
it's an adversarial
setting where you

00:35:30.211 --> 00:35:32.100
also need to worry about
the possibility of

00:35:32.101 --> 00:35:34.540
an adversary polluting or

00:35:34.541 --> 00:35:37.275
actively evading
your classifier and so on.

00:35:37.276 --> 00:35:39.530
So, we have some
preliminary ideas here.

00:35:39.531 --> 00:35:44.275
We talked about how we can
build a robust representation,

00:35:44.276 --> 00:35:45.990
a finite state machine
representation

00:35:45.991 --> 00:35:48.020
of different IOT devices

00:35:48.021 --> 00:35:49.390
and use that to

00:35:49.391 --> 00:35:52.795
flag deviations for
many different kinds of devices.

00:35:52.796 --> 00:35:55.880
The idea here is how do we
actually make it robust to

00:35:55.881 --> 00:35:59.600
polluted incoming data
and how do we actually

00:35:59.601 --> 00:36:01.450
build a compact behavioral
model that's actually

00:36:01.451 --> 00:36:04.510
hard for an adversary to aid
in the future and so on.

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:09.735
So, another interesting problem

00:36:09.736 --> 00:36:11.585
domain I've been thinking about,

00:36:11.586 --> 00:36:13.710
I don't actually have
a pretty slide for it,

00:36:13.711 --> 00:36:16.235
but the vision here
is think about,

00:36:16.236 --> 00:36:19.075
it really came about

00:36:19.076 --> 00:36:20.800
reading a bunch of
news articles, read this.

00:36:20.801 --> 00:36:25.085
It turns out if you use a
service like Airbnb or a hotel,

00:36:25.086 --> 00:36:26.780
what looks like
a thermostat is actually

00:36:26.781 --> 00:36:29.350
a camera or a listening device
that's spying on you.

00:36:29.351 --> 00:36:31.530
So, now, the question
then is like, okay,

00:36:31.531 --> 00:36:32.905
I'm a user of these services,

00:36:32.906 --> 00:36:35.415
I want to be able to walk into
a room and say, hey, look,

00:36:35.416 --> 00:36:37.450
and sort of map out the kinds of

00:36:37.451 --> 00:36:39.830
sensors and capabilities that
this particular room has,

00:36:39.831 --> 00:36:42.385
and say, hey, this camera is
actually listening to me.

00:36:42.386 --> 00:36:44.590
That's a thermostat or that
looks like a smoke alarm,

00:36:44.591 --> 00:36:45.860
but it's actually
a camera that has

00:36:45.861 --> 00:36:47.480
listening capabilities and so on.

00:36:47.481 --> 00:36:48.960
So, the idea here
is I should be able

00:36:48.961 --> 00:36:50.060
to walk into a room and say,

00:36:50.061 --> 00:36:52.610
figure out the capabilities
of individual devices

00:36:52.611 --> 00:36:53.910
inside that particular room so

00:36:53.911 --> 00:36:55.260
I actually know what
I'm getting into.

00:36:55.261 --> 00:36:56.350
If I'm going at Airbnb,

00:36:56.351 --> 00:36:58.290
I know what I'm getting
into as a hidden camera

00:36:58.291 --> 00:37:01.225
there so maybe I'll be careful
about what I do there.

00:37:01.226 --> 00:37:04.440
So, these are interesting
application of machine learning.

00:37:04.441 --> 00:37:06.250
You can think about
I can walk in,

00:37:06.251 --> 00:37:08.130
I can monitor different
kinds of features.

00:37:08.131 --> 00:37:09.600
I can monitor the Wi-Fi,

00:37:09.601 --> 00:37:11.845
the Bluetooth,
the energy spectrum.

00:37:11.846 --> 00:37:15.240
I can monitor the RF spectrum.

00:37:15.241 --> 00:37:18.245
I can monitor visual aids image
and so on and so forth.

00:37:18.246 --> 00:37:19.600
I have a bunch of
different features

00:37:19.601 --> 00:37:21.805
and I want to be able to say,

00:37:21.806 --> 00:37:23.460
hey, this is features,

00:37:23.461 --> 00:37:25.065
is that a camera or
is that a thermostat?

00:37:25.066 --> 00:37:26.235
That gets a thermostat,

00:37:26.236 --> 00:37:28.070
claims to be a thermostat,
looks like a thermostat,

00:37:28.071 --> 00:37:30.040
but it's EM footprint
looks like it looks like

00:37:30.041 --> 00:37:31.800
a camera or it's Wi-Fi

00:37:31.801 --> 00:37:34.530
footprint looks like
a camera. Can I do it?

00:37:34.531 --> 00:37:36.720
Maybe I can, and it turns
out there's actually

00:37:36.721 --> 00:37:39.250
some interesting
preliminary work by

00:37:39.251 --> 00:37:41.320
some other folks from

00:37:41.321 --> 00:37:42.600
this paper that they were looking

00:37:42.601 --> 00:37:44.460
at and actually
have this data set.

00:37:44.461 --> 00:37:46.375
So, those of you looking
at for interesting

00:37:46.376 --> 00:37:48.020
IOT smart home data set is

00:37:48.021 --> 00:37:50.215
actually seemed like
a nice data set.

00:37:50.216 --> 00:37:53.510
They collected traffic emitted
by 31 smart home devices

00:37:53.511 --> 00:37:57.410
and very standard traffic
fingerprinting techniques.

00:37:57.411 --> 00:37:59.180
Look at the average packet size.

00:37:59.181 --> 00:38:00.620
Look at the kind of
protocols,the kind of

00:38:00.621 --> 00:38:03.935
codes these particular
devices is sending,

00:38:03.936 --> 00:38:05.480
and use that to create a compact

00:38:05.481 --> 00:38:06.855
fingerprint and say, hey, look,

00:38:06.856 --> 00:38:08.255
if you're sending a lot of

00:38:08.256 --> 00:38:10.535
outgoing connections,
you're probably a camera.

00:38:10.536 --> 00:38:12.380
Or if you're sending
periodic heartbeats,

00:38:12.381 --> 00:38:14.310
you're probably Amazon Alexa
and so on and so forth.

00:38:14.311 --> 00:38:16.890
I mean nothing fancy there.

00:38:20.120 --> 00:38:22.260
Good question. I
don't know. So, then

00:38:22.261 --> 00:38:24.030
maybe after this, put
some other features.

00:38:24.031 --> 00:38:27.660
So, the question is what
features can you monitor?

00:38:27.661 --> 00:38:29.720
Maybe there's actually
manifestations

00:38:29.721 --> 00:38:31.280
in the other parts as well.

00:38:31.281 --> 00:38:36.000
Maybe there's an
electromagnetic emissions

00:38:36.001 --> 00:38:38.570
I actually manifests even
though it's wired and so on.

00:38:38.571 --> 00:38:40.230
Or you can actually
also think about like

00:38:40.231 --> 00:38:41.570
actively doing something to see

00:38:41.571 --> 00:38:44.530
how they respond to
active request and queries.

00:38:44.531 --> 00:38:47.605
I don't know. This is
an open question for me.

00:38:47.606 --> 00:38:49.810
Actually, I've been
trying to implement this

00:38:49.811 --> 00:38:52.560
using ProtoNN, which
is like a cool

00:38:52.561 --> 00:38:54.090
new compact machine
learning algorithm

00:38:54.091 --> 00:38:55.445
that you guys have developed.

00:38:55.446 --> 00:38:57.130
Actually, over one day,

00:38:57.131 --> 00:39:00.455
and Harsha has been helping
me actually get this running.

00:39:00.456 --> 00:39:02.405
Results are TBD
because I haven't,

00:39:02.406 --> 00:39:04.695
my coding skills are rusty.

00:39:04.696 --> 00:39:06.915
All right. So that's

00:39:06.916 --> 00:39:08.580
interesting applications
of machine learning

00:39:08.581 --> 00:39:09.965
to security and privacy.

00:39:09.966 --> 00:39:11.615
Last part, I actually do
want to talk about like

00:39:11.616 --> 00:39:14.985
this new big center that
we're actually doing.

00:39:14.986 --> 00:39:17.430
Looking at these problems in
a more general perspective,

00:39:17.431 --> 00:39:20.420
I talked about monitoring
for telemetry,

00:39:20.421 --> 00:39:22.370
interesting applications of IOT

00:39:22.371 --> 00:39:23.480
to machine learning and security,

00:39:23.481 --> 00:39:25.290
setup a machine-learning
IOT security and

00:39:25.291 --> 00:39:27.410
privacy, but more broadly,

00:39:27.411 --> 00:39:30.960
the goal here is thinking
about there is a lot of

00:39:30.961 --> 00:39:33.729
interesting new next
generation applications

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:35.415
of IOT drones,

00:39:35.416 --> 00:39:39.415
self-driving cars, and
so on and so forth that

00:39:39.416 --> 00:39:42.230
essentially require
new capabilities

00:39:42.231 --> 00:39:43.660
inside the network
from the systems

00:39:43.661 --> 00:39:45.315
that we're developing and so on.

00:39:45.316 --> 00:39:47.300
So the idea here,
the vision here is

00:39:47.301 --> 00:39:49.570
that so there's a whole bunch
of new applications and

00:39:49.571 --> 00:39:51.600
unit network
infrastructures that are

00:39:51.601 --> 00:39:53.950
going to be
fundamentally changing

00:39:53.951 --> 00:39:56.050
the way we need to
rethink how we have done

00:39:56.051 --> 00:39:59.335
classic systems design and
machine learning and so on.

00:39:59.336 --> 00:40:01.405
So the new network
infrastructure,

00:40:01.406 --> 00:40:03.235
and this is the project
called CONIX.

00:40:03.236 --> 00:40:05.070
The vision here is there are
going to be extreme scale,

00:40:05.071 --> 00:40:06.545
think about smart cities,

00:40:06.546 --> 00:40:10.240
pervasive augmented
reality applications

00:40:10.241 --> 00:40:12.560
and so on that are going to
have a lot of variability,

00:40:12.561 --> 00:40:13.830
heterogeneity,

00:40:13.831 --> 00:40:16.550
cool spatiotemporal
context-aware application.

00:40:16.551 --> 00:40:18.810
Then they're going to require
low latency communication,

00:40:18.811 --> 00:40:20.210
otherwise, their car
is going to crash

00:40:20.211 --> 00:40:22.360
and and so on and so forth.

00:40:22.770 --> 00:40:25.680
So our work, the goal of

00:40:25.681 --> 00:40:27.630
the sector is basically
saying that we can

00:40:27.631 --> 00:40:29.580
think about classical paradigms

00:40:29.581 --> 00:40:31.800
of billions of edge devices,

00:40:31.801 --> 00:40:33.650
the cloud or
the Internet, and so on.

00:40:33.651 --> 00:40:35.520
But really none of them are

00:40:35.521 --> 00:40:37.460
a good fit for
these emerging class of

00:40:37.461 --> 00:40:39.450
applications and we need to

00:40:39.451 --> 00:40:41.850
bring some more structure
to this chaos,

00:40:41.851 --> 00:40:43.690
and in particular we
actually believe that

00:40:43.691 --> 00:40:45.010
the network infrastructure are

00:40:45.011 --> 00:40:47.160
the middle of this network,

00:40:47.161 --> 00:40:49.100
the middle layer is going to play

00:40:49.101 --> 00:40:50.160
a much bigger reward than it's

00:40:50.161 --> 00:40:52.590
classically played in the system.

00:40:53.770 --> 00:41:02.400
Sorry. So that's a good question.

00:41:02.401 --> 00:41:04.990
So one thing about all
self-driving cars are actually

00:41:04.991 --> 00:41:07.580
having massive GPUs and
stuff inside the system.

00:41:07.581 --> 00:41:11.270
So, think about like can

00:41:11.271 --> 00:41:13.160
you make the Tata Nano
a self-driving car?

00:41:13.161 --> 00:41:14.660
Can you have a low cost
self-driving car,

00:41:14.661 --> 00:41:16.180
but actually the computer
actually back-end?

00:41:16.181 --> 00:41:18.120
It's not like we
have bazillions have

00:41:18.121 --> 00:41:20.430
GPUs inside your network,
inside a car and so on.

00:41:20.431 --> 00:41:22.850
That's one example where
you can think about you can

00:41:22.851 --> 00:41:26.105
democratize the ability of
doing it by doing it more.

00:41:26.106 --> 00:41:27.740
That's one way of
thinking about it

00:41:27.741 --> 00:41:29.430
but low cost is not
the only thing.

00:41:29.431 --> 00:41:32.560
That's one sort of natural way
of thinking about it.

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:38.440
Yeah, and the goal here is
the vision of this having

00:41:38.441 --> 00:41:41.185
more in-network computational
capabilities as

00:41:41.186 --> 00:41:43.270
a natural way of dealing with

00:41:43.271 --> 00:41:45.685
a bunch of these different
distributed applications,

00:41:45.686 --> 00:41:47.500
and it's again given

00:41:47.501 --> 00:41:49.315
that the cloud and the edge
are both had to change,

00:41:49.316 --> 00:41:53.075
the network actually might
be the way to do things.

00:41:53.076 --> 00:41:56.300
So, again, this is
not a single systems

00:41:56.301 --> 00:41:58.785
or a machine learning
or a hardware problem.

00:41:58.786 --> 00:42:01.600
It's really a cross-cutting
problem where, as I said,

00:42:01.601 --> 00:42:02.950
there's a very diverse team from

00:42:02.951 --> 00:42:05.520
multiple universities we're
working towards this vision.

00:42:05.521 --> 00:42:08.105
You're looking at machine
learning for resilience,

00:42:08.106 --> 00:42:09.390
building these trustworthy

00:42:09.391 --> 00:42:11.575
computation systems from scratch,

00:42:11.576 --> 00:42:13.605
safe secure and
smart programming.

00:42:13.606 --> 00:42:17.360
Building cool new spatio-temporally
aware applications,

00:42:17.361 --> 00:42:20.825
better in-network coordination
and control mechanisms,

00:42:20.826 --> 00:42:22.680
as well as new
hardware architectures

00:42:22.681 --> 00:42:23.930
and maybe even some of

00:42:23.931 --> 00:42:25.880
the compact machine
learning algorithms

00:42:25.881 --> 00:42:27.480
that can run at the edge
and so on again.

00:42:27.481 --> 00:42:28.780
We're in early days,

00:42:28.781 --> 00:42:31.775
it's really less than six months
since we launched.

00:42:31.776 --> 00:42:34.870
So this is a long-term effort

00:42:34.871 --> 00:42:37.055
that we're actually
launching right now.

00:42:37.056 --> 00:42:39.140
So research fellows or other

00:42:39.141 --> 00:42:41.490
people in this room who are
applying for grad school,

00:42:41.491 --> 00:42:44.020
we are hiring aggressively.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:47.840
Cool. That's it. I'm done

